{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act criminal-conviction eviction grounds","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or civilly held liable for, assault or threats, theft, illegal drug activity, or human trafficking, or who knowingly allows such a person to reside there; suit must be filed within two years.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Tenants of residential dwelling units covered by the Eviction Law; these grounds appear in subsections (o) through (r).","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found to be delinquent by the Court due to an offense involving theft of property from the landlord or from tenants residing in the same building or complex.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"In an owner-occupied two- or three-family dwelling, a landlord may evict only by court order and only for holding over, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, willful damage, or rule or lease violations, with three-month, one-month, or three-day notice as specified.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house good-cause eviction rule","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses, i.e. buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations (personal occupancy and removal from residential use)","requirement":"A landlord who evicts to personally occupy must occupy at least six months or owes the tenant triple damages plus attorney fees; one who boards up or demolishes must avoid residential use for five years, or owes treble damages and up to $10,000.00.","key_value":"3x damages + attorney fees; civil penalty up to $10,000.00","coverage_conditions":"Evictions carried out after a notice stating the landlord intends to personally occupy the unit, or to board up, demolish, or permanently retire the property from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Triple damages plus attorney fees and costs to the former tenant; civil penalty up to $10,000.00 per violation; property may not be registered as a planned real estate development for five years.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"If instead the landlord permits personal occupancy of the unit by another tenant or registration of conversion of the property to a condominium or cooperative, the landlord is liable to the former tenant for three (3) times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6(a)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-reprisal protection for tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, substantially alter the lease, or refuse to renew in reprisal because a tenant enforced lease or legal rights, complained to a government authority about health or safety violations, joined a tenant organization, or refused a retaliatory lease change.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings excluded"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction protections (90-day notice to quit)","requirement":"Foreclosure is not grounds for eviction. Before evicting a tenant, a buyer who wants to occupy the unit must give a 90-day notice to quit, and a lease running beyond 90 days must be honored. New owners must notify tenants within 10 business days.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of residential rental units in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and owner-occupied two- and three-family units are not exempt.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units after foreclosure"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90-day notice to quit when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"The text states federal law does not preempt State or local laws giving tenants longer time periods or additional protections.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy (40-year protection)","requirement":"Tenants who are at least 62, permanently disabled, or qualifying disabled veterans, and who lived in the building at least one year before the conversion is recorded, may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is within the statutory limits.","key_value":"40-year protected tenancy","coverage_conditions":"Tenants in buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership who meet the age, disability, veteran, and one-year residency requirements.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 (qualified-county conversion protections)","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Act of 1992 extends protected-tenancy protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties who were not eligible under the 1981 Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act; at present the only qualified county is Hudson County.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Tenants in buildings converted or being converted in qualified counties; currently only Hudson County qualifies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Converted buildings in qualified counties (currently Hudson County)"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on rent increases above rent control or unconscionable increases","requirement":"A landlord may not increase rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or, where the property is not subject to rent control, may not impose an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental units; the item appears in a list of prohibited landlord practices, with rent-controlled properties governed by the local ordinance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.4,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
